## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamental TDD understanding and ability to follow red-green-refactor cycle - essential for writing maintainable code at Anthropic.

## Expected Answer
Candidate should explain: 1) Write failing test (red), 2) Write minimal code to pass (green), 3) Improve code while tests pass (refactor). Should demonstrate writing test first, then implementation.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Red: Write failing test
test('calculator adds numbers', () => {
  const calc = new Calculator();
  expect(calc.add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
});

// Green: Minimal implementation
class Calculator {
  add(a: number, b: number): number {
    return a + b;
  }
}

// Refactor: Extract if needed
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test edge cases like negative numbers?
- When would you skip TDD in a real project?
- How do you handle testing async code in TDD?

## Why This Is Asked
This checks practical ability to design reliable, beginner-friendly tests for a time-based data pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Dependency injection for test doubles
- Time control to remove nondeterminism
- In-memory mocks vs real services
- Flake detection and CI gates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Jest example with in-memory queue and fixed time
const { processNext } = require('../src/worker');
const InMemoryQueue = require('../src/mocks').InMemoryQueue;
const redisApi = require('../src/redis');

jest.mock('../src/redis');

test('processNext deterministic time', () => {
  const queue = new InMemoryQueue(['action1','action2']);
  redisApi.getQueue.mockReturnValue(queue);
  jest.useFakeTimers('modern');
  jest.setSystemTime(new Date('2026-01-01T12:00:00Z'));
  processNext();
  expect(queue.processed).toBe(true);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle multiple worker instances with separate queues?
- What metrics would indicate flaky tests and how would you triage them?
